          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What actually happens in the airways during an asthma flare-up?",
          "answer": "Asthma is a long-term condition in which the airway lining is inflamed, and the muscle around the airways tends to tighten. This narrows the airways and makes breathing harder. The narrowing comes and goes, which is why symptoms vary from day to day and within a day. (Source: *Asthma*, condition summary.)\n\nThe reference material I searched does not explain the detailed steps of a flare-up. It does not describe how mucus production works, and it does not explain airway remodeling beyond the basic idea. I can't fill those gaps from memory, so I can't give you a reliable plain-language account of them here. A clinician or a patient education resource from a respiratory charity would be a good next step.\n\nIf someone is struggling to breathe, speaking in short phrases, or their lips or face look blue or grey, call emergency services now. Keep them sitting upright and use any reliever inhaler exactly as their own action plan says. (Source: *Severe difficulty breathing*, emergency guidance.)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
